WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.658
90 percent of all land plants

00:00:02.658 --> 00:00:05.570
need to have a&nbsp;plant-fungus-root association.

00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:09.920
You can see the fungus, which exists
in most of its life as thin&nbsp;filaments,

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:12.782
and the network of thin filaments, 
we call it mycelium.

00:00:12.782 --> 00:00:15.910
And the mycelium of the&nbsp;fungus
 can tap into the root of plants

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:17.558
and make a symbiosis.

00:00:17.558 --> 00:00:21.666
And thereby, they exchange&nbsp;nutrients that
 the other one isn't so good at making

00:00:21.666 --> 00:00:22.930
or capturing.

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:24.343
But it doesn’t stop&nbsp;there,

00:00:24.343 --> 00:00:27.639
because the fungi can tap into 
other plants at the same time,

00:00:27.639 --> 00:00:31.029
So what you end up with is this 
massive underground network

00:00:31.029 --> 00:00:32.678
mediated by fungi.

00:00:32.678 --> 00:00:35.162
Now, it's not just nutrients that are flowing,

00:00:35.162 --> 00:00:36.599
but also communication,

00:00:36.599 --> 00:00:41.135
because plants can talk to each 
other through the fungal network

00:00:41.135 --> 00:00:44.836
to create chemical signals to&nbsp;be able 
to warn of a pest attack, for example.

